;; What a board actually brought up, printed at the end of boot.
;;
;; Compose this onto a board image and the console shows, once, every node the
;; device tree describes: which bound to a driver, which did not, and which the
;; tree says to leave alone. That is the state of a bring-up on one screen, and
;; it is the difference between "it booted" and knowing what came up.
;;
;; It is the runtime counterpart of silt dt --linux, which predicts the same
;; thing from the tree and the kernel source without booting anything. When the
;; two disagree, one of them is wrong in a way worth knowing:
;;
;;   silt predicts a driver, the board says NO DRIVER
;;     the symbol is set and the driver did not probe - check the hardware,
;;     the clocks, the regulators
;;
;;   the board binds something silt did not predict
;;     the scanner's Makefile parsing missed it; a bug in silt, not the image
;;
;; Needs nothing exotic. /sys/firmware/devicetree comes from CONFIG_OF, which
;; any board booting from a device tree already has, and the of_node symlinks
;; come with it.

(fragment feature:board-demo
  (doc "enumerate the device tree at boot and print what bound")

  (buildroot
    (path-append BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY "overlay"))

  (linux
    ;; The tree the kernel was given, published back. Without this the script
    ;; has nothing to read and says so rather than reporting an empty board.
    (y CONFIG_OF)))
